<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_member-functions">
  <title>Member Functions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A function name by itself does not always tell us enough about the object
      performing the action. For example, the action of <c>cook()</c> is very
      general and may be even ambiguous without knowing a speccific cooking instrument(object).
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
cook(); // Too general
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      But different objects can perform that action in different ways. These
      expressions provide more context:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
oven.cook();
grill.cook();
stove.cook();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The function name is the same, but the object before the dot tells us
      which object is performing the behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      In C++, functions that belong to an object are called
      <term>member functions</term>. Member functions let us associate
      behavior with the type of object that naturally performs that behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the previous section, functions operated on structure objects by
      receiving those objects as parameters.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, a function could read a <c>Rectangle</c> without modifying
      it:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle) {
    return rectangle.length * rectangle.width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Or a function could modify the original object by receiving it by
      reference:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle) {
    rectangle.length *= 2.0;
    rectangle.width *= 2.0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      These are <term>free functions</term>: they exist separately from the
      structure definition, and the object is supplied as an argument.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can now move behavior that naturally belongs to a
      <c>Rectangle</c> into the structure itself.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Adding Behavior to a Structure</title>

    <p>
      Consider the familiar <c>Rectangle</c> structure:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We can place a function inside the structure definition:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const {
        return length * width;
    }
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The function <c>calculate_area</c> is now a member function of
      <c>Rectangle</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We call it using the dot operator:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

double area = room.calculate_area();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Compare the two forms:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
calculate_area(room);      // free function

room.calculate_area();     // member function
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      In the free-function version, the <c>Rectangle</c> object is supplied
      as an argument. In the member-function version, the object before the
      dot provides the context for the operation.
    </p>

    <p>
      This does not mean that member functions are always better than free
      functions. The member-function form is useful when the operation
      naturally belongs to the object.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Accessing Data Members Inside a Member Function</title>

    <p>
      A member function can directly access the data members of the object on
      which it was called.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We do not write <c>room.length</c> or <c>room.width</c> inside the
      member function. The member function must work for every
      <c>Rectangle</c> object, not only for an object named <c>room</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
Rectangle table{6.0, 3.0};

double room_area = room.calculate_area();
double table_area = table.calculate_area();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      When <c>room.calculate_area()</c> runs, <c>length</c> and
      <c>width</c> refer to the data members of <c>room</c>.
      When <c>table.calculate_area()</c> runs, they refer to the data
      members of <c>table</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.calculate_area()

room
+------------------+
| length : 12.0    |
| width  : 8.0     |
+------------------+

calculate_area uses:
length -> 12.0
width  -> 8.0
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The object that calls a member function determines which object's data
      members the function uses.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Member Functions That Modify an Object</title>

    <p>
      A member function can also change the state of the object on which it
      is called.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the previous section, we used a free function:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle) {
    rectangle.length *= 2.0;
    rectangle.width *= 2.0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      As a member function, the same behavior can be written:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    void double_size() {
        length *= 2.0;
        width *= 2.0;
    }
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Then:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

room.double_size();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      changes the state of <c>room</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
before                         after
room                           room
+------------------+           +------------------+
| length : 12.0    |           | length : 24.0    |
| width  : 8.0     |           | width  : 16.0    |
+------------------+           +------------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Notice that the member function does not need a
      <c>Rectangle&amp;</c> parameter. The object before the dot is already
      the object being modified.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>const Member Functions</title>

    <p>
      In the previous section, you used <c>const</c> references to indicate
      that a function should read an object without modifying it:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Member functions can make a similar promise.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area() const;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The <c>const</c> after the parentheses means that the member function
      promises not to modify the object on which it is called.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const {
        return length * width;
    }
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Because <c>calculate_area</c> only reads <c>length</c> and
      <c>width</c>, marking it <c>const</c> expresses the function's intent.
    </p>

    <p>
      A <c>const</c> member function cannot modify the object's data members.
      For example, this would not compile:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area() const {
    length = 20.0;   // error
    return length * width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      By contrast, <c>double_size</c> changes the object, so it is not marked
      <c>const</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
void double_size() {
    length *= 2.0;
    width *= 2.0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      A useful way to think about the difference is:
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Reading and Changing Object State</title>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><b>Member function</b></cell>
          <cell><b>Changes the object?</b></cell>
          <cell><b>Trailing const?</b></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>calculate_area()</c></cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>calculate_perimeter()</c></cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>double_size()</c></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>More Than One Member Function</title>

    <p>
      A structure can contain both data and several related behaviors.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const {
        return length * width;
    }

    double calculate_perimeter() const {
        return 2.0 * (length + width);
    }

    void double_size() {
        length *= 2.0;
        width *= 2.0;
    }
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The structure now groups together:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          state: <c>length</c> and <c>width</c>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          behavior that observes the state:
          <c>calculate_area()</c> and <c>calculate_perimeter()</c>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          behavior that changes the state: <c>double_size()</c>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This is an important idea in C++: a structure can contain both data and
      functions that naturally operate on that data.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Defining Member Functions Outside the Structure</title>

    <p>
      Small member functions can be defined directly inside a structure, as
      we have done so far. Member functions can also be declared inside the
      structure and defined later.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
    double calculate_perimeter() const;
    void double_size();
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The definitions can appear later in the file:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}

double Rectangle::calculate_perimeter() const {
    return 2.0 * (length + width);
}

void Rectangle::double_size() {
    length *= 2.0;
    width *= 2.0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The <c>Rectangle::</c> before each function name tells C++ that the
      function belongs to the <c>Rectangle</c> type.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <c>::</c> operator is called the
      <term>scope resolution operator</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the complete single-file examples in this book, we will usually place
      structure definitions and member-function declarations before
      <c>main</c>, and place the member-function definitions after
      <c>main</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Complete Example</title>

    <listing label="chapter5_member_functions_AC_1">
      <title>
        This program uses both observing and state-changing member functions.
      </title>

      <program interactive="activecode"
               line-numbers="yes"
               label="chapter5_member_functions_AC_1_editor">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
    double calculate_perimeter() const;
    void double_size();
};

int main() {
    Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

    std::cout << "Area: "
              << room.calculate_area() << '\n';

    std::cout << "Perimeter: "
              << room.calculate_perimeter() << '\n';

    room.double_size();

    std::cout << "New area: "
              << room.calculate_area() << '\n';

    return 0;
}

double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}

double Rectangle::calculate_perimeter() const {
    return 2.0 * (length + width);
}

void Rectangle::double_size() {
    length *= 2.0;
    width *= 2.0;
}
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      The object before the dot determines which <c>Rectangle</c> the member
      function operates on.
    </p>

    <p>
      The observing member functions are marked <c>const</c>, while
      <c>double_size</c> is not because it modifies the object's state.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Free Function or Member Function?</title>

    <p>
      Member functions are useful when an operation naturally belongs to a
      particular object.
    </p>

    <p>
      The real-world example from the beginning showed this idea:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
oven.cook();
grill.cook();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The object gives the behavior additional context. Similarly:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.calculate_area();
room.double_size();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      clearly associate those operations with <c>room</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This does not mean that every function should become a member function.
      Free functions are still useful, especially when an operation does not
      naturally belong to one particular object.
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful design question is:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Does this operation naturally belong to this object?
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      We will return to this question later in the chapter as we design
      classes and their interfaces.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_member_functions_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which expression correctly calls the <c>calculate_area</c> member
          function on an object named <c>room</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>calculate_area(room)</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is the syntax for the earlier free-function version.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><c>room.calculate_area()</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Member functions are called using the object, the dot operator,
              and the member-function name.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>Rectangle.calculate_area(room)</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The member function is called on a specific object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_member_functions_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why can <c>calculate_area()</c> use <c>length</c> and
          <c>width</c> without receiving a <c>Rectangle</c> parameter?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              All <c>Rectangle</c> objects share the same <c>length</c> and
              <c>width</c>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Each <c>Rectangle</c> object has its own data members.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              A member function operates in the context of the object on which
              it was called.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The calling object determines which data members the member
              function uses.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The data members automatically become global variables.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Data members belong to individual objects; they are not global
              variables.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_member_functions_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which member function should normally be marked with trailing
          <c>const</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              A function that calculates and returns an object's area without
              changing the object.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A member function that only observes object state can promise
              not to modify the object by using trailing <c>const</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              A function that doubles the object's length and width.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A state-changing member function cannot make the same read-only
              promise.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Every member function, whether it changes the object or not.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Trailing <c>const</c> is appropriate when the member function
              does not modify the object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_member_functions_4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does <c>Rectangle::</c> mean in this definition?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It creates a new <c>Rectangle</c> object.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              No object is being created in this function definition.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It identifies <c>calculate_area</c> as a member function of
              <c>Rectangle</c>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The scope resolution operator associates the definition with the
              <c>Rectangle</c> type.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It passes a <c>Rectangle</c> to the function by reference.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>::</c> is the scope resolution operator; it is not reference
              syntax.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_member_functions_5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the member-function definition below, what do <c>length</c> and
          <c>width</c> refer to?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The data members of the <c>Rectangle</c> object on which
              <c>calculate_area()</c> was called.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Inside a member function, data members of the calling object can
              be accessed directly without writing the object's name.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Global variables named <c>length</c> and <c>width</c>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              These names refer to data members of the calling object, not
              global variables.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Function parameters that C++ creates automatically.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              They are not parameters. They are data members of the calling
              object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_member_functions_6"
              number="yes"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Arrange the blocks to define <c>Robot::needs_recharge()</c> outside
          the structure. The function should return <c>true</c> when the
          battery level is below 20 and should not modify the object.
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
struct Robot {
    int battery_level;
    bool needs_recharge() const;
};
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>bool Robot::needs_recharge() const {</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="2">
          <cline>    return battery_level &lt; 20;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="3">
          <cline>}</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>bool needs_recharge(Robot robot) {</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>    return robot.battery_level &lt; 20;</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
